WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Practical Methods for Estimating Defect Opportunities

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.266
Welcome to this session on defect opportunities.

3
00:00:08.266 --> 00:00:14.699
What we have in front of us is a simple concept called defects per opportunity.

4
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.700
That's located out here in the far right-hand column.

5
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:23.600
That's our defects per opportunity. So, I’ll label that

6
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.300
defects per opportunity.

7
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:33.200
Now, along here in the numerator position, we have defects.

8
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.766
And in the denominator position, we have opportunities.

9
00:00:38.766 --> 00:00:41.799
So, the numerator contains the defects

10
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:47.000
and the denominator contains the opportunities.

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Now with that, what we’re looking at is each occurrence of a defect opportunity.

12
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.100
So this example, is for a certain CTQ,

13
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:05.100
CTQ dash eighteen, just for the sake of argument.

14
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:10.500
It’s just going to be known as CTQ 18.

15
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.900
Make our discussion easy.

16
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:20.833
Now given that, let's take a look at this block right here, that I’m now changing the color on.

17
00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:25.599
To a fairly loud color there.

18
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:32.566
That one says, that opportunity was brought into existence.

19
00:01:32.566 --> 00:01:39.032
That's what a one signifies, the opportunity was brought into existence.

20
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:45.633
Now if in fact, it had not been brought into existence, then a zero goes there.

21
00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:52.599
Let's suppose the process cycle started, but was not completed, therefore a zero goes there.

22
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.900
So, what we’re talking about here, is the process cycle.

23
00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.900
And I’m going to make that simple for us.

24
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:05.300
We’ll represent a cycle by the letter C.

25
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:12.266
And we’ll say that this is cycle 1, 2, 3,

26
00:02:12.266 --> 00:02:17.132
4. Cycle 1, 2, 3, 4 of the process.

27
00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:23.566
Where the outcome either completed the cycle or it did not. Zero did not complete the cycle.

28
00:02:23.566 --> 00:02:30.432
Therefore, if I have a zero in the denominator for any given cycle.

29
00:02:30.433 --> 00:02:35.633
It's impossible to have a one here. That would have to be zero.

30
00:02:35.633 --> 00:02:39.633
Cause you see, if it didn't cycle, it didn't create a product.

31
00:02:39.633 --> 00:02:46.466
If it didn't create a product, it could not have a defect. Therefore, a zero has to go there.

32
00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:52.432
Simple way to think about it. Now, in this case,

33
00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:57.166
let's suppose, notice I arrange the scenario.

34
00:02:57.166 --> 00:03:02.499
Let's suppose that we cycled the process four times.

35
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:09.500
And of those four times, we had two successful cycles. Something was produced.

36
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.833
Of those two successful cycles,

37
00:03:13.833 --> 00:03:19.066
cycle 2 and cycle 3, one had a defect.

38
00:03:19.066 --> 00:03:24.166
Therefore, we produced

39
00:03:24.166 --> 00:03:30.966
one defect out of two opportunities, or my defects per opportunity is .50.

40
00:03:30.966 --> 00:03:36.232
That's an easy way to say that. Easy way to look at that.

41
00:03:36.233 --> 00:03:41.233
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
